t are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev )\nL137: \nL138: [Input] Core recommender toggle\nL139: \nL140: CORE Recommender (cite35†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL141: \nL142: [Input] IArxiv recommender toggle\nL143: \nL144: IArxiv Recommender (cite36†What is IArxiv?†iarxiv.org )\nL145: \nL146:   * Author\nL147:   * Venue\nL148:   * Institution\nL149:   * Topic\nL150: \nL151: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL152: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL153: \nL154: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL155: \nL156: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL157: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite37†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL158: \nL159: cite38†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite39†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL160: \nL161: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite40†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL162: cite41†About†info.arxiv.org · cite42†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite43†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite44†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite45†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L163: \nL164: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.16229v1] Q-MLLM: Vector Quantization for Robust Multimodal Large Language Model Security (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16229v1)\nciteturn6582view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16229v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Q-MLLM: Vector Quantization for Robust Multimodal Large Language Model Security\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Wei Zhao , cite6†Zhe Li , cite7†Yige Li , cite8†Jun Sun L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Q-MLLM: Vector Quantization for Robust Multimodal Large Language Model Security, by Wei Zhao and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have demonstrated impressive capabilities in cross-modal understanding, but remain vulnerable to adversarial attacks through visual inputs despite robust textual safety mechanisms. These vulnerabilities arise from two core weaknesses: the continuous nature of visual representations, which allows for gradient-based attacks, and the inadequate transfer of text-based safety mechanisms to visual content.\nL17: We introduce Q-MLLM, a novel architecture that integrates two-level vector quantization to create a discrete bottleneck against adversarial attacks while preserving multimodal reasoning capabilities. By discretizing visual representations at both pixel-patch and semantic levels, Q-MLLM blocks attack pathways and bridges the cross-modal safety alignment gap. Our two-stage training methodology ensures robust learning while maintaining model utility.\nL18: Experiments demonstrate that Q-MLLM achieves significantly better defense success rate against both jailbreak attacks and toxic image attacks than existing approaches. Notably, Q-MLLM achieves perfect defense success rate (100\\%) against jailbreak attacks except in one arguable case, while maintaining competitive performance on multiple utility benchmarks with minimal inference overhead.\nL19: This work establishes vector quantization as an effective defense mechanism for secure multimodal AI systems without requiring expensive safety-specific fine-tuning or detection overhead. Code is available at cite11†this https URL†github.com .\nL20: Comments:  | Accepted by NDSS 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.16229 [cs.CR]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.16229v1 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16229†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Wei Zhao [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 10:55:19 UTC (1,086 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Q-MLLM: Vector Quantization for Robust Multimodal Large Language Model Security, by Wei Zhao and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CR\nL44: \nL45: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L46: \nL47: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite23†cs L52: cite24†cs.AI L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: B